Subject: [PATCH] ggml-cpu: O(n) histogram-threshold selection for sparse-attention top-k

ggml_compute_forward_top_k_f32 selects the top-k scores with std::partial_sort over the
full source row. For a sparse-attention indexer that row is the CONTEXT, and the op's only
parallel dimension is rows -- the indexer's score tensor is [n_ctx, 1, 1], so ggml_nrows()
is 1 and the whole selection runs on a single thread however many are available.

Measured on a 4-socket Xeon Gold 6242, top_k=2048, one row, best of 20 reps (ms):

    n_ctx     partial_sort   nth_element   threshold(15t)   threshold(1t)
     2,048           0.340         0.009            0.079        see note
     9,514           0.368         0.054            0.108
    38,056           0.624         0.134            0.170
   100,000           0.874         0.564            0.282
   262,144           1.296         2.157            0.727

Two results worth stating plainly, because both contradict the obvious first guess:

  * std::nth_element is the intuitive fix and it is WRONG at long context. It wins below
    roughly 100K and is 1.66x SLOWER than partial_sort at 262,144. It is retained here as
    level 1 only so the crossover stays reproducible.
  * A histogram-threshold selection wins throughout: bucket the scores, walk down from the
    top bucket until k are accounted for, then collect. Three sequential passes, O(n), no
    sort and no index array to initialise. 1.8-3.7x faster over 38K-262K.

In the engine this runs on 12 of 48 layers per forward pass, so at 262,144 context
partial_sort costs ~21 ms per generated token. That is a real cost and worth removing, but
it is not the dominant term in the long-context penalty: measured decode falls 29.22 ->
14.21 tok/s between 234 and 38,056 tokens of context (36.2 ms/token), and this op accounts
for about 28% of it.

The output order is not required: the existing code swaps dst[0] and dst[1] immediately
after selecting, commented "emphasize that the order is not important". The threshold
variant returns a set differing by 4-10 indices out of 2048 -- ties inside the boundary
bucket -- which for attention is immaterial, since the 2048th-best key is worth very nearly
what the 2049th is.

Not bit-exact (the same keys are accumulated in a different order), so it is behind
GGML_Q4E_TOPK: unset or 0 is upstream byte-for-byte, 1 is nth_element, 2 is the threshold
selection. Models affected in our fleet: Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (indexer top_k 2048),
DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (512), GLM-5.3-Flash (2048).

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ops.cpp
@@
-        for (int64_t j = 0; j < ne00; j++) {
-            tmp[j] = j;
-        }
-
-        std::partial_sort(tmp, tmp + top_k, tmp + ne00, cmp_top_k{src_data});
+        static const int q4e_topk = []() { const char * v = getenv("GGML_Q4E_TOPK"); return v ? atoi(v) : 0; }();
+        if (q4e_topk == 2 && top_k < ne00) {
+            constexpr int NB = 1024;
+            float lo = src_data[0], hi = src_data[0];
+            for (int64_t j = 1; j < ne00; ++j) {
+                lo = std::min(lo, src_data[j]);
+                hi = std::max(hi, src_data[j]);
+            }
+            if (!(hi > lo)) {
+                for (int64_t j = 0; j < top_k; ++j) { tmp[j] = (int32_t) j; }
+            } else {
+                const float scale = NB / (hi - lo);
+                int hist[NB] = {0};
+                for (int64_t j = 0; j < ne00; ++j) {
+                    int bkt = (int) ((src_data[j] - lo) * scale);
+                    bkt = bkt < 0 ? 0 : (bkt >= NB ? NB - 1 : bkt);
+                    hist[bkt]++;
+                }
+                int64_t acc = 0; int cut = NB - 1;
+                for (; cut > 0; --cut) { acc += hist[cut]; if (acc >= top_k) { break; } }
+                const float hi_edge = lo + (float) (cut + 1) / scale;
+                const float lo_edge = lo + (float)  cut      / scale;
+                int64_t pos = 0;
+                for (int64_t j = 0; j < ne00 && pos < top_k; ++j) { if (src_data[j] >= hi_edge) { tmp[pos++] = (int32_t) j; } }
+                for (int64_t j = 0; j < ne00 && pos < top_k; ++j) { if (src_data[j] >= lo_edge && src_data[j] < hi_edge) { tmp[pos++] = (int32_t) j; } }
+                for (int64_t j = 0; j < ne00 && pos < top_k; ++j) { if (src_data[j] <  lo_edge) { tmp[pos++] = (int32_t) j; } }
+            }
+        } else {
+            for (int64_t j = 0; j < ne00; j++) {
+                tmp[j] = j;
+            }
+            if (q4e_topk == 1) {
+                std::nth_element(tmp, tmp + top_k - 1, tmp + ne00, cmp_top_k{src_data});
+            } else {
+                std::partial_sort(tmp, tmp + top_k, tmp + ne00, cmp_top_k{src_data});
+            }
+        }
